1
00:00:00,404 --> 00:00:03,698
Start at vertex D in Monday's drawing.

2
00:00:03,698 --> 00:00:07,553
Following arrows reaches E, F, and G.

3
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:10,417
But reaching somewhere, returning from somewhere,

4
00:00:10,417 --> 00:00:13,226
and going through somewhere are different questions.

5
00:00:14,158 --> 00:00:18,620
Both class periods used depth-first search to make those differences visible.

6
00:00:19,571 --> 00:00:21,719
Open Figure six point four.

7
00:00:21,719 --> 00:00:26,667
Above is a directed graph: vertices joined by one-way arrows.

8
00:00:26,667 --> 00:00:29,271
Below are bars recording the search.

9
00:00:29,271 --> 00:00:32,722
Depth-first search follows an unexplored neighbor,

10
00:00:32,722 --> 00:00:35,921
finishes that exploration, then returns.

11
00:00:36,824 --> 00:00:39,204
Let's connect the arrows to the bars.

12
00:00:40,137 --> 00:00:44,890
A bar opens when the recursive call begins and closes when it returns.

13
00:00:44,890 --> 00:00:48,210
The left endpoint is the starting time, called pre.

14
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:51,618
The right endpoint is the finishing time, called post.

15
00:00:52,511 --> 00:00:55,828
A call stays active while it waits for calls below it.

16
00:00:56,718 --> 00:01:00,846
Vertex H starts at six and finishes at seventeen.

17
00:01:00,846 --> 00:01:03,997
Vertex D's whole interval fits inside it.

18
00:01:03,997 --> 00:01:06,628
So does vertex L's.

19
00:01:06,628 --> 00:01:08,694
Which one finishes first?

20
00:01:14,637 --> 00:01:17,668
And can D still be active when L starts?

21
00:01:23,545 --> 00:01:26,119
Read the endpoints before answering.

22
00:01:27,011 --> 00:01:30,553
D finishes at eight, before L starts at nine.

23
00:01:30,553 --> 00:01:33,456
Both are descendants of H in the search tree.

24
00:01:33,456 --> 00:01:37,489
Recursive calls nest or finish before another begins.

25
00:01:38,437 --> 00:01:43,551
Their active intervals cannot partly overlap like two sliding windows.

26
00:01:44,504 --> 00:01:49,009
The solid arrows record which call first discovered each vertex.

27
00:01:49,009 --> 00:01:52,499
They form a forest: one or more search trees.

28
00:01:52,499 --> 00:01:55,165
The other arrows still exist in the graph.

29
00:01:56,104 --> 00:02:01,035
A visited mark prevents another recursive call, not another incoming edge.

30
00:02:01,958 --> 00:02:05,617
A vertex is new, active, or finished.

31
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:10,135
An arrow back to an active ancestor closes a directed cycle:

32
00:02:10,135 --> 00:02:13,178
the search already followed a path down to us.

33
00:02:14,098 --> 00:02:17,940
An arrow to a finished vertex need not close a cycle.

34
00:02:17,940 --> 00:02:20,866
‘Visited’ alone hides that distinction.

35
00:02:21,791 --> 00:02:24,200
The book names four edge types.

36
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:26,687
Tree edges discover children.

37
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:29,669
Forward edges lead to other descendants.

38
00:02:29,669 --> 00:02:32,323
Back edges lead to ancestors.

39
00:02:32,323 --> 00:02:36,278
Cross edges connect different branches or trees.

40
00:02:37,237 --> 00:02:42,042
The timestamps let you recognize these
relationships without trusting how the graph is

41
00:02:42,042 --> 00:02:42,911
drawn.

42
00:02:43,824 --> 00:02:49,801
In a graph without directed cycles, reading
finish order backward puts every vertex before

43
00:02:49,801 --> 00:02:51,520
its outgoing neighbors.

44
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:53,495
That's a topological order.

45
00:02:54,451 --> 00:02:58,070
For a recurrence whose arrows point to needed subproblems,

46
00:02:58,070 --> 00:03:03,132
evaluate in the opposite order: finish the needed values before using them.

47
00:03:04,058 --> 00:03:06,258
Monday's graph has cycles.

48
00:03:06,258 --> 00:03:11,779
A, B, C can all reach one another; D and E can return to each other;

49
00:03:11,779 --> 00:03:13,637
so can F and G.

50
00:03:14,519 --> 00:03:18,985
These maximal return-trip groups are strongly connected components.

51
00:03:18,985 --> 00:03:21,068
Collapse them into boxes.

52
00:03:21,068 --> 00:03:24,007
The box graph has no directed cycles.

53
00:03:24,924 --> 00:03:29,937
If several boxes formed a cycle, we could travel around it and return.

54
00:03:29,937 --> 00:03:33,371
Those boxes would belong to one larger component.

55
00:03:34,291 --> 00:03:38,874
This is why we can order the components even
though we cannot topologically order the

56
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:40,407
original vertices.

57
00:03:41,311 --> 00:03:46,477
Does one DFS from D collect exactly its strongly connected component?

58
00:03:52,391 --> 00:03:54,605
Trace every reachable arrow.

59
00:03:54,605 --> 00:03:58,251
It reaches D, E, F, and G.

60
00:03:58,251 --> 00:04:00,034
That's two components.

61
00:04:00,034 --> 00:04:06,320
A DFS tree records discovery; a strongly
connected component requires return journeys.

62
00:04:07,258 --> 00:04:11,659
Starting in F-G visits both vertices and stays within their group.

63
00:04:11,659 --> 00:04:15,839
No edge points to another component, so this is a sink component.

64
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:18,556
Record F and G as assigned.

65
00:04:19,504 --> 00:04:25,165
A search from D then collects D and E,
skipping the vertices already recorded.

66
00:04:26,091 --> 00:04:29,854
Reverse the arrows and run DFS over every vertex,

67
00:04:29,854 --> 00:04:32,185
recording vertices when they finish.

68
00:04:32,185 --> 00:04:38,174
Our stack is B, C, A, vertex E, D, G, F.

69
00:04:38,174 --> 00:04:40,736
Return to the original graph and pop.

70
00:04:41,658 --> 00:04:45,590
F comes first, then the next unassigned start is D,

71
00:04:45,590 --> 00:04:46,550
then A.

72
00:04:47,478 --> 00:04:51,163
Reversing arrows swaps source and sink components.

73
00:04:51,163 --> 00:04:55,564
Decreasing finishing time selects source components of the reversed graph,

74
00:04:55,564 --> 00:04:57,660
which are sinks of the original.

75
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:00,417
Popping already reverses finishing order.

76
00:05:01,345 --> 00:05:04,857
That explains Monday's ‘no need to reverse’ note.

77
00:05:05,791 --> 00:05:11,572
Across an arrow between distinct components, the
upstream component has the greater maximum

78
00:05:11,572 --> 00:05:12,914
finishing time.

79
00:05:12,914 --> 00:05:17,233
If explored first, it finishes after exploring downstream.

80
00:05:18,124 --> 00:05:23,749
If downstream is explored first, it cannot
reach upstream and finishes earlier.

81
00:05:23,749 --> 00:05:27,066
That's the ordering fact to watch for in the proof.

82
00:05:32,498 --> 00:05:35,649
Start fresh discovery state for pass two.

83
00:05:35,649 --> 00:05:41,248
Keep one assigned set across all components,
but a new list for each component.

84
00:05:41,248 --> 00:05:44,604
Each pass checks vertices and edges once.

85
00:05:45,571 --> 00:05:49,565
That's linear time: big O of V plus E.

86
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:54,176
Your notes mention counting paths to and from vertices,

87
00:05:54,176 --> 00:05:57,379
multiplying those counts, and removing vertices.

88
00:05:57,379 --> 00:06:03,508
The chapter’s nearby exercise asks which
vertices every source-to-target path crosses.

89
00:06:04,424 --> 00:06:08,513
Together, these suggest counting paths through each vertex,

90
00:06:08,513 --> 00:06:11,205
then comparing with all complete paths.

91
00:06:12,085 --> 00:06:16,187
Use our three-box DAG as a graph in its own right.

92
00:06:16,187 --> 00:06:20,210
Call the first box the start and the last the destination.

93
00:06:20,210 --> 00:06:23,475
‘To’ counts paths from the start to a chosen box.

94
00:06:24,495 --> 00:06:27,825
‘From’ counts paths from that box to the destination.

95
00:06:28,771 --> 00:06:34,943
Every complete path through the box splits
uniquely into one prefix and one suffix.

96
00:06:34,943 --> 00:06:39,344
In a DAG, joining them cannot repeat another vertex:

97
00:06:39,344 --> 00:06:41,098
that would create a cycle.

98
00:06:42,024 --> 00:06:46,526
So paths through equals paths to multiplied by paths from.

99
00:06:47,445 --> 00:06:49,880
Give the start one empty prefix.

100
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:52,979
Add incoming counts in topological order.

101
00:06:52,979 --> 00:06:58,457
Give the destination one empty suffix,
then add outgoing counts in reverse order.

102
00:06:59,391 --> 00:07:02,946
The middle box has one path to it and one from it.

103
00:07:02,946 --> 00:07:04,583
Their product is one.

104
00:07:05,491 --> 00:07:09,254
There are two complete routes: directly across the shortcut,

105
00:07:09,254 --> 00:07:10,634
or through the middle.

106
00:07:10,634 --> 00:07:12,167
How many go through the middle?

107
00:07:18,052 --> 00:07:19,289
One of the two.

108
00:07:19,289 --> 00:07:21,098
Now remove the shortcut.

109
00:07:21,098 --> 00:07:24,289
One route remains, and it passes through the middle.

110
00:07:24,289 --> 00:07:29,559
Comparing these counts tells us whether every route uses that vertex.

111
00:07:35,024 --> 00:07:39,803
Now switch to an undirected graph: connections work both ways.

112
00:07:39,803 --> 00:07:41,873
Remove F from this picture.

113
00:07:41,873 --> 00:07:44,943
G becomes separated from the other vertices.

114
00:07:45,891 --> 00:07:51,139
A vertex whose removal disconnects a
connected graph is called a cut vertex.

115
00:07:51,139 --> 00:07:54,377
We can study that change using a DFS tree.

116
00:07:55,311 --> 00:07:57,447
Start DFS at F.

117
00:07:57,447 --> 00:08:01,457
One child branch explores the five vertices on the left.

118
00:08:01,457 --> 00:08:03,397
Another explores G.

119
00:08:03,397 --> 00:08:07,183
Could an edge join these branches without passing through F?

120
00:08:13,091 --> 00:08:17,710
If it did, DFS would have followed it and
discovered them in the same branch.

121
00:08:18,637 --> 00:08:24,458
That's why an undirected DFS root is a cut
vertex exactly when it has at least two children

122
00:08:24,458 --> 00:08:26,007
in the DFS tree.

123
00:08:26,007 --> 00:08:29,194
Count tree children, not graph neighbors.

124
00:08:30,124 --> 00:08:35,577
With only one child, the remaining tree
stays connected after removing the root.

125
00:08:36,530 --> 00:08:42,116
For a non-root vertex, look for a connection
from a child subtree to an ancestor above

126
00:08:42,116 --> 00:08:42,767
it.

127
00:08:42,767 --> 00:08:46,709
That connection gives the subtree another route to the rest of the graph.

128
00:08:47,624 --> 00:08:53,949
If one child subtree has no such connection,
removing the vertex separates that subtree.

129
00:08:54,865 --> 00:08:59,761
Read section six point one asking what the bars reveal about ancestry.

130
00:08:59,761 --> 00:09:05,751
In six point two and six point three, connect
back edges to cycles and finish order to

131
00:09:05,751 --> 00:09:06,828
schedules.

132
00:09:07,771 --> 00:09:12,103
In six point four, connect those schedules to cached answers.

133
00:09:13,024 --> 00:09:17,777
Then read six point five and the two-pass algorithm in six point six:

134
00:09:17,777 --> 00:09:23,490
why do return-trip groups form a DAG, and why
does the next search stay inside one group?

135
00:09:24,490 --> 00:09:29,437
Finish with Exercises three and four: count paths through vertices,

136
00:09:29,437 --> 00:09:32,663
then study what changes when a vertex is removed.

137
00:09:33,604 --> 00:09:37,953
DFS records who discovered whom and when each call finished.

138
00:09:37,953 --> 00:09:41,638
Those records explain the chapter's bars, dependency orders,

139
00:09:41,638 --> 00:09:43,604
and component searches.

140
00:09:43,604 --> 00:09:47,520
Today's removal questions ask whether another route exists.

141
00:09:48,458 --> 00:09:53,992
On paper, draw the root's two child branches
and explain what remains when you remove

142
00:09:53,992 --> 00:09:54,913
the root.
